WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.038
This is our Intelligent Task Offloading project, and this white box is the USRP

00:00:06.038 --> 00:00:07.180
radio behind it.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.438
First we start the base station using srsRAN.

00:00:12.438 --> 00:00:18.580
It detects the radio, comes up on band n78, and connects to the 5G core.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.089
Then we check the core network, which is Open5GS.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:28.714
Every service here, like the AMF for access and the UPF that carries user data,

00:00:28.714 --> 00:00:32.060
shows active and running, so the core is ready.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.943
Now the edge layer.

00:00:34.943 --> 00:00:40.882
Kubernetes is running nine edge nodes plus a scheduler, and each one serves an image

00:00:40.882 --> 00:00:41.660
classifier.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.944
This next one's the gateway.

00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:47.750
It's the brain of the system, it decides where every task should run.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.786
And this server tracks live throughput, because the best choice depends on how much

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:56.380
bandwidth we've got right now, and that keeps changing all the time.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.521
Over on the cloud side, we've got a FastAPI service in Docker, which acts as a

00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:04.982
backup whenever the edge is overloaded.

00:01:04.982 --> 00:01:10.218
And finally, this Node server stores the results, so we can use them for retraining

00:01:10.218 --> 00:01:11.480
the models later on.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.675
Now let's look at the user side.

00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:19.927
This phone has our programmable SIM, so it isn't on a normal mobile network, it's

00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:22.001
connected to our own private 5G.

00:01:22.001 --> 00:01:26.151
Think of it as our own small mobile operator, just for this lab.

00:01:26.151 --> 00:01:31.597
We open the browser and run a search, and the results load, which tells us real data

00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:36.460
is moving all the way through the radio, the core, and out to the internet.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.403
Okay, now for the actual test.

00:01:40.403 --> 00:01:45.876
From the laptop, we send a picture to the gateway as a classification request, and

00:01:45.876 --> 00:01:51.350
the system has to work out where that job should run, at the edge or in the cloud.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.144
The gateway runs an optimization called MILP.

00:01:56.144 --> 00:02:01.804
It looks at node load, bandwidth and the deadline, then picks the option with the

00:02:01.804 --> 00:02:03.830
lowest latency for that task.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.600
And here's the answer, right in the response.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.764
It chose edge node two, because that has the lowest latency and still meets the one

00:02:12.764 --> 00:02:13.760
second deadline.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.861
It estimates about 0.93 seconds at the edge, against 1.83 in the cloud, so the edge

00:02:21.861 --> 00:02:22.940
wins easily.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.530
The model looked at the picture and said it's a dog, which is right.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:32.212
And now, over here on the storage server, you can see the file arriving, with its

00:02:32.212 --> 00:02:33.830
name right there in the log.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.559
We open the uploads folder, and there's our picture, exactly as we sent it.

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:45.848
In our testing, this approach cut latency by about 56 percent, compared to using

00:02:45.848 --> 00:02:46.840
only the cloud.
